<section class="fcd-section" id="keyvault">
    <h2>Azure Key Vault</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Both apps read <code>KEY_VAULT_URL</code> and authenticate with <code>EnvironmentCredential</code>, which uses <code>AZURE_TENANT_ID</code>, <code>AZURE_CLIENT_ID</code> and <code>AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET</code> environment variables (a service principal / app registration).</li>
        <li>The website both registers Key Vault as a <em>configuration provider</em> (so every secret is also visible through <code>IConfiguration</code>) and keeps a <code>SecretClient</code> for direct reads. Many secrets are re-read on demand (for example the SMTP credentials on every email send and <code>Email-Target</code> on every contact message), so a rotated secret takes effect without a restart in those places.</li>
        <li><code>KEY_VAULT_URL</code> is required even in Development. Without it the website stops at start-up.</li>
        <li>The service principal needs <strong>Get</strong> (and optionally List) on secrets, and <strong>Wrap Key / Unwrap Key / Get</strong> on the key named by <code>data-key</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The complete list of secret names and what each one is for is in <a href="/docs/system/configuration#secrets">Configuration → Key Vault secrets</a>.</p>
    <div class="fcd-note fcd-note--warn"><p>Least privilege: the website and the email app share one vault and, in practice, one principal. Consider a separate principal per app, each limited to the secrets it needs, and rotating <code>AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET</code> on a schedule.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="fcd-section" id="dataprotection">
    <h2>ASP.NET Data Protection keys</h2>
    <p>Data Protection is the framework that encrypts and signs the authentication cookie, antiforgery tokens, the session cookie, Identity's email and reset tokens, the encrypted IDs in URLs, and the email callback tokens. Its configuration:</p>
    <div class="fcd-table-wrap">
        <table class="fcd-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Production</th><th>Development</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Key ring location</td><td>Azure Blob Storage: secrets <code>blob-connection-string</code>, <code>blob-container-name</code>, <code>blob-file-name</code> (one XML blob)</td><td><code>DataProtectionKeys/</code> folder in the working directory</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Key encryption at rest</td><td>Each key is wrapped with the Key Vault key whose identifier is in secret <code>data-key</code></td><td>None (plain XML on disk)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Key lifetime</td><td colspan="2">7 days. A new key becomes active automatically; old keys stay in the ring to decrypt older data.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Application name</td><td colspan="2"><code>ForestChurches-App</code> (isolates this app's keys; keep it the same across deployments)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Algorithms</td><td colspan="2">Framework defaults: AES-256-CBC for encryption with HMACSHA256 for authenticity</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <div class="fcd-note fcd-note--danger">
        <span class="fcd-note__title">If the key ring is lost or changed</span>
        <p>Everyone is signed out, all outstanding invitation/confirmation/reset links stop working, and old encrypted church/event links show "not found". No stored data is lost, because database data isn't encrypted with these keys. If the Key Vault protector can't be configured, the site logs a warning and continues with unwrapped keys in Blob Storage, so check the start-up log for <em>"DataProtection: Configured Azure Key Vault protector."</em></p>
    </div>
    <div class="fcd-note fcd-note--warn"><p>The repository contains a <code>DataProtectionKeys/</code> folder with development key files. Those keys must never be used in production. If the repository is public, treat them as exposed.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="fcd-section" id="tokens">
    <h2>Callback tokens in emails</h2>
    <p>Most links in emails don't point straight at a page. <code>MailSender.GenerateUniqueToken</code> builds a <code>CallbackToken</code> object (random UID, email, expiry, redirect URL), serialises it to JSON, encrypts it with the Data Protection purpose <code>"User  Registration"</code> and base64url-encodes it. The email links to <code>/ValidateToken?token=…</code>, which decrypts it, checks the expiry and redirects to the stored URL. Because the redirect target is inside the encrypted token, it can't be tampered with.</p>
    <div class="fcd-table-wrap">
        <table class="fcd-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Email</th><th>Token valid for</th><th>Then lands on</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>You're Invited</td><td>24 h</td><td>Registration page for that email</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Reset Password</td><td>2 h (the Identity reset code inside is also 2 h)</td><td>Reset password page</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Event Created / Event Deleted / Do you still need this?</td><td>24 h</td><td>My Events</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Your Requested Information</td><td>24 h</td><td>The church's contact form</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Confirm your account / Confirm New Email</td><td>Link goes directly to the confirm page; the Identity token inside lasts 2 h</td><td>Confirm email page</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>Tokens aren't stored or marked used, so a token can be opened any number of times until it expires. What it protects (registration, reset) has its own single-use checks.</p>
    <p>There's also an older <code>/callback/{token}</code> controller with the same logic; nothing links to it.</p>
</section>

<section class="fcd-section" id="jwt">
    <h2>Website ↔ email app authentication (JWT)</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>For each email, the website's <code>JwtTokenGenerator</code> creates an HMAC-SHA256 JWT valid for 10 minutes, signed with Key Vault secret <code>jwt-token-securityKey</code>, issuer <code>jwt-token-issuer</code>, audience <code>jwt-token-audience</code>.</li>
        <li>The email app validates issuer <code>ForestChurches</code>, audience <code>F0rest_Ch3rches</code>, lifetime and signature. The signing key it checks against is <strong>hardcoded in the email app's <code>Program.cs</code></strong>, not read from Key Vault. Sending only works while the Key Vault value and the hardcoded value are identical, and the key is visible to anyone who can read that repository.</li>
        <li>The email app calls its own <code>POST /Email/send</code> endpoint with a JWT it signs itself using the Key Vault value.</li>
        <li>The website also registers a JWT bearer scheme with the same settings, but no website endpoint uses it.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="fcd-note fcd-note--warn"><p>To fix: make the email app read the signing key, issuer and audience from Key Vault, then rotate <code>jwt-token-securityKey</code> (the current value is in Git history).</p></div>
</section>

<section class="fcd-section" id="cookies">
    <h2>Cookies and sessions</h2>
    <div class="fcd-table-wrap">
        <table class="fcd-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Cookie</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Settings</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>.AspNetCore.Identity.Application</code></td><td>Sign-in</td><td>HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=None, 14 days if "keep me signed in", no sliding renewal</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Identity.External</code> / <code>Identity.TwoFactorUserId</code></td><td>External sign-in and 2FA steps</td><td>Secure, SameSite=None, short-lived</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>X-CSRF-TOKEN-COOKIE</code></td><td>Antiforgery</td><td>HttpOnly; header name <code>X-CSRF-TOKEN-HEADER</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>.AspNetCore.Session</code></td><td>Server session (ChurchSuite PKCE state, TempData)</td><td>Secure, SameSite=Lax, 30-minute idle timeout; in-memory session store, so sessions are lost on restart and aren't shared between replicas</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>.AspNetCore.Culture</code></td><td>Chosen language</td><td>1 month, readable by script, SameSite=Lax</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>fc-theme</code></td><td>Light/dark choice</td><td>Set by script</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>The site uses essential cookies only; there are no analytics or advertising cookies.</p>
</section>

<section class="fcd-section" id="http">
    <h2>HTTP protections</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>HTTPS and HSTS</strong>: <code>UseHttpsRedirection</code>; HSTS max-age 1 year with includeSubDomains and preload (sent by custom middleware on every response and by <code>UseHsts</code> outside Development). <code>Server</code> and <code>X-Powered-By</code> headers are removed.</li>
        <li><strong>CSRF</strong>: Razor Pages validate antiforgery tokens on form posts. A custom middleware also validates every POST/PUT/DELETE except <code>/Identity/Account/Login</code>, <code>Register</code>, <code>Logout</code>, <code>ExternalLogin</code>, <code>/dist</code> and <code>/api</code>. (Those Identity pages still get Razor Pages' own validation.)</li>
        <li><strong>File-extension blocker</strong>: requests whose path contains a dot and whose extension isn't an allowed static type (css, js, images, fonts) get 403. It runs after <code>UseStaticFiles</code>, so it only affects requests that didn't match a file in <code>wwwroot</code>; files in <code>wwwroot</code> (including uploaded bulletin PDFs) are served regardless.</li>
        <li><strong>CORS</strong>: a policy for <code>https://forestchurches.co.uk</code> is defined but not applied (<code>UseCors</code> isn't called), so cross-origin browser requests get no CORS headers.</li>
        <li><strong>Content Security Policy</strong>: <code>CSPNonceMiddleware</code> exists but isn't registered, so <strong>no CSP header is sent</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Rate limiting</strong>: none. Sign-in, the contact form and search aren't throttled, and lockout is off.</li>
        <li><strong>Uploads</strong>: images go to Blob Storage under generated names; bulletin PDFs go to <code>wwwroot/uploads/bulletins</code>. Admin-only.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

