<section class="fcd-section" id="gaps">
    <h2>Security gaps to fix</h2>
    <p>Ordered by severity. Each one is also on the <a href="/docs/system/operations#issues">known-issues list</a> with what to change.</p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>ChurchSuite sign-in can be pointed at any account.</strong> <code>/churchsuite/authorize?user_id=…</code> accepts a user ID without checking who is signed in and stores it in the session. When the OAuth callback (<code>/signin-churchsuite</code>) completes and nobody is signed in, it signs the browser in <em>as that user ID</em>. Anyone who learns a user's ID and has any ChurchSuite login could sign in as that user, including a SuperAdmin. The same pattern exists in <code>/ChurchSuite/Authorize</code> and <code>Callback</code>. Until fixed, consider disabling these routes.</li>
        <li><strong>Live logs are broadcast without sign-in.</strong> <code>/logsHub</code> has no <code>[Authorize]</code>, and <code>LogWatcherService</code> pushes every new log row (which can include email addresses and request details) to all connected clients.</li>
        <li><strong>Secrets in the repository</strong>: an <code>AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET</code> in <code>Properties/launchSettings.json</code>, a Google API key in <code>appsettings.Development.json</code>, a GitHub Actions secret <em>name</em> in <code>deploy.yml</code> that looks like a client secret value, the email app's hardcoded JWT key, plus <code>DataProtectionKeys/</code> and support zips. Rotate all of these and remove them from history.</li>
        <li><strong>Plain-text third-party credentials in the database</strong>: ChurchSuite access and refresh tokens (<code>ChurchSuiteCredentials</code>) and per-church <code>ChurchSuiteApiKey</code> (<code>ChurchInformation</code>). Encrypt with Data Protection or move to Key Vault.</li>
        <li><strong>The Google API key is handed to any visitor</strong> by <code>/Church?handler=GoogleMapsApiKey</code>. Restrict the key by HTTP referrer and API in Google Cloud, or use a separate browser key.</li>
        <li><strong>No account lockout and no rate limiting</strong> on sign-in, so passwords can be guessed without limit (the 12-character policy helps).</li>
        <li><strong>No Content Security Policy</strong> (middleware not registered). The contact-form message is inserted into the email HTML without encoding.</li>
        <li><strong>Registration needs only a whitelisted address</strong>, not the token. See <a href="/docs/system/accounts#whitelist">Accounts</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Email app</strong>: Swagger UI is public; SMTP uses STARTTLS <em>when available</em>, so it would fall back to plain text if the server didn't offer TLS; the <code>Template</code> field is a file path chosen by the caller (only a valid-JWT caller, so low risk).</li>
        <li><strong>Open redirect potential</strong>: <code>redirect_uri</code> on the ChurchSuite routes is used for redirects without checking it's a local URL.</li>
        <li><code>/oauth-debug</code> returns the caller's own claims. It reveals nothing about other users but should be removed from production.</li>
    </ol>
</section>
